    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7249",
      "problem": "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ The matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14156741/assets/029f87cdb01f386b7025d7c250afc52cf46ddd2468c5185892ad8fdb2724a946.svg)\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & ={b}_{2}+{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14156741/assets/c79f9d608dab28affc7dd2dc338e3a216e669a8dbdb2f07d7d21032a5d3b0b95.svg)\n\nNow, the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14156741/assets/2eac16ba2d79a4964ee86564b863f1716844469882f30d3f3b5b5f85abf8ba54.svg)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=1,$ $c=0$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+0 = 2.\n$$",
